1 NOTE Darius I, called The Great (558?-486 bc), king of Persia (521?-486 bc), 
2 CONT son of the Persian noble Hystaspes (588-521 bc), and a member of a royal 
2 CONT Persian family, the Achaemenids. In 522 bc, on the death of King Cambyses 
2 CONT II, a group of Magian priests tried to give the throne to one of their 
2 CONT number, the usurper Gaumata; he pretended to be Smerdis (died about 523 
2 CONT bc), the murdered brother of Cambyses II. In 521, Darius defeated Gaumata 
2 CONT and was chosen king of Persia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The first two years of his reign were occupied with suppressing 
2 CONT rebellions, the most important of which occurred in Babylonia. Thereafter 
2 CONT he devoted himself to reforming the internal organization of Persia and 
2 CONT making its outer borders secure. He reorganized the vast empire into 20 
2 CONT satrapies, built highways, organized a postal system, reformed the 
2 CONT currency, encouraged commerce, and won the goodwill of large portions of 
2 CONT the heterogeneous population. Because he respected their religions, he was 
2 CONT honored by the Jews, whom he permitted to complete the rebuilding of the 
2 CONT Temple at Jerusalem in 516; by the Egyptians, whose high priest he 
2 CONT consulted; and by the Greeks of Asia Minor, whose oracles supported him 
2 CONT during the revolt of the Greek cities. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In protecting the borders of the empire, Darius conquered new territories 
2 CONT along the Indus River in the east and in the Caucasus Mountains in the 
2 CONT northeast, but his expedition in 516 against the tribes of the Danube 
2 CONT River failed. In 499 a revolt broke out among the Ionian Greek cities of 
2 CONT Asia Minor, partly encouraged by some of the Greek cities on the mainland. 
2 CONT The revolt was suppressed by 493, and Darius prepared to punish the 
2 CONT mainland Greeks for their intervention. In 492 an army under Mardonius 
2 CONT (flourished 500-479 bc), the son-in-law of Darius, crossed the Bosporus 
2 CONT into Thrace but was unable to reach Greece because the supply ships were 
2 CONT wrecked off Mount Athos. Two years later, a strong Persian force under the 
2 CONT joint command of Artaphernes (flourished 5th century bc), a nephew of 
2 CONT Darius, and the Mede commander Datis (flourished 5th century bc) invaded 
2 CONT Greece from the north but was defeated at Marathon. A third expedition was 
2 CONT being prepared when Darius died. He left a detailed account of his reign, 
2 CONT inscribed in three languages on a towering rock. This Behistun 
2 CONT Inscription, the first English transcription of which was complete in 
2 CONT 1849, confirms many details of the life of Darius.
