2 CONT Adad-nirari IIIFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search Adad-nirari III King of Assyria
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tell al-Rimah Stele was discovered in 1967 and commemorates Adad-nirari III’s campaigns in the West.[1] Father Shamshi-Adad V Mother Shammuramat Children Ashur-nirari V Shalmaneser IV Ashur-dan III
2 CONT
2 CONT Adad-nirari III (also Adad-narari) was a King of Assyria from 811 to 783 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] FamilyAdad-nirari was a son and successor of king Shamshi-Adad V and was apparently quite young at the time of his accession, because for the first five years of his reign his mother Shammuramat[2] acted as regent, which ma
2 CONC y have given rise to the legend of Semiramis.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is widely rejected that his mother acted as regent, but she was surprisingly influential for the time period.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a father of kings Ashur-nirari V, Shalmaneser IV and Ashur-dan III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tiglath-Pileser III described himself as a son of Adad-nirari in his inscriptions, but it is uncertain if this is truthful.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] BiographyAdad-nirari's youth, and the struggles his father had faced early in his reign, caused a serious weakening for the Assyrian rulership over Mesopotamia, and gave way to the ambitions of the most officers, governors 
2 CONC and the local rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Adad-nirari's inscriptions, he led several military campaigns with the purpose of regaining the strength Assyria enjoyed in the times of his grandfather Shalmaneser III.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the eponym canon, he campaigned in all directions until the last of his 28 years of reign, and he was the builder of the temple of Nabu at Nineveh. Among his actions was a siege of Damascus in the time of Ben-Hadad II
2 CONC I in 796 BCE, which led to the eclipse of the Aramaean Kingdom of Damascus and allowed the recovery of Israel under Jehoash (who paid the Assyrian king tribute at this time) and Jeroboam II. Additionally, he is thought by some to 
2 CONC be the "King of Nineveh" who, upon receiving Jonah's prophecy of forthcoming doom, dressed himself in sackcloth and ordered a fast throughout the city in a successful attempt to prevent it. (Jonah 3:6–9)
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of Adad-nirari's vigor, Assyria entered a several decades long period of weakness following his death.
2 CONT
