1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shammuramat
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semiramis
2 CONT
2 CONT The historical Assyrian queen Shammuramat (Semiramis), wife of Shamshi-Adad V of Assyria, certainly existed. After her husband's death, she served as regent from 810 - 806 BC for her son, Adad-nirari III. Shammuramat would have th
2 CONC us been briefly in control of the vast Neo Assyrian Empire, which 150 years later stretched from the Caucasus Mountains in the north to the Arabian Peninsula in the south, and western Iran in the east to Cyprus in the west. ￼
2 CONT
2 CONT Georges Roux speculated that the later Greek and Indo-Iranian (Persian and Median) flavoured myths surrounding Semiramis stem from successful campaigns she waged against these peoples, and the novelty of a woman ruling such an emp
2 CONC ire. 
